    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d382">

                <pb facs="0804" n="790" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v1/pg790"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Beutwell, Secretary of the Treasury, to Mr. Fish,
                        Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Treasury
                        Department</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1869-06-24"
                            ><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 24, 1869</date>.</dateline>
                <p><seg type="margin">[66]</seg>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: Our detectives inform us that a
                    vessel *is fitting out in Philadelphia with munitions of war, and taking on
                    board a considerable number of men destined to aid the revolutionists in Cuba.
                        <pb facs="0805" n="791" xml:id="pg791"/> We have not been able to learn the
                    name of the vessel, but two men who represent themselves as enlisted for the
                    service propose furnishing information if they can be compensated for it.</p>
                <p>Upon the information I have received, I have directed the collector of customs at
                    Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, to make such search as is in his power for the
                    purpose of ascertaining the facts.</p>
                <p>I am very truly, yours, &amp;c., &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>GEO. S. BOUTWELL,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of the
                            Treasury.</hi></signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hamilton Fish</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
